      "id": "DC_kwDOKrJqmc4BFxTh",
      "body": "I've been working on the same R500L5 (SDX55) and wanted to share what I've found and fixed so far. I could use some guidance on the rest.  I'd rather not open a large PR on a half-finished transport if there's a simpler path.\r\n\r\nThe R500L5's diag data plane is bound to a disconnected USB gadget (`diag_mdm`). The control plane works over QRTR, but `/dev/diag` drops modem-bound log traffic because there's no USB host cable. That's why rayhunter installs and runs but never sees `0x10` LTE RRC/ML1 frames — empty pcaps, same as @simeononsecurity saw.\r\n\r\nThere are two separate problems here. One is the message transport:\r\n\r\n`DIAG_IOCTL_SWITCH_LOGGING` failure (the `-1` error):\r\nThe X55/4.14 driver wants a 24-byte `diag_logging_mode_param_t`, not the legacy 9-byte one. The combined multi-proc switch (`device_mask=0x3`) fails because proc 1's re-translation of the already-switched proc 0 returns `-EINVAL`. Fix: two sequential per-proc ioctls on one fd (proc 0 with `APSS|MPSS`, then proc 1). Verified on device: `diag-init` completes, logging mode reaches MULTI (3), session mask `0x81` on both procs.\r\n\r\n`capacity overflow` panic in `MessagesContainer`:\r\nThe parser treated every response as `[data_type][num_data][{len,data}...]`, so the modem's `0x100 DCI_LOG_MASKS` / `0x200 DCI_EVENT_MASKS` responses (mask tables, not message lists) read a garbage count and overflowed. Replaced with a bounded parser that also skips the `remote_token` prefix. Five unit tests; the daemon now idles cleanly through init.\r\n\r\n## Still broken — where I need guidance\r\n\r\nThe data plane itself. I built a QRTR (`AF_QIPCRTR`) transport to the modem diag command service (node 3 / port 14) with EFS2 read/write, planning to flip the DCI gate (`/nv/item_files/services/diag/DCI_disable` to 0). But:\r\n\r\n- EFS2 reads of `DCI_disable` still return `ENOENT`, and a write to `DCI_disable.conf` \"reports\" success with no effect.\r\n- No DCI MHI channel in the device tree, so it's unclear the data plane can come up at all without firmware-side changes.\r\n\r\nOpen questions:\r\n\r\n1. Kernel/DT omission (fixable in the driver) or modem firmware limitation (dead end)?\r\n2. Is QRTR + EFS2 the right lever, or is there a known-correct way to bring up the X55 data plane?\r\n3. Worth a PR at all — and if so, split (small parser fix first, transport as follow-up)? The full change is ~950 lines which is well over the 400-line contribution guidance... and the transport half is still experimental.\r\n\r\nBranch is at `cturner/rayhunter` on `r500l5-x55-diag` if anyone wants to look.",
      "author": {
        "login": "cturner",
        "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/88383?u=21af7e55adf6e00eaca0b0c58ac4384bf6c94141&v=4",
        "url": "https://github.com/cturner"
      },
